      sources: "Sources: Learn, Manage group Managed Service Accounts (retrievers as a security group). Chapter 06, “The code,” “The inputs.”",
      say: `So what does the code add? A module, two entry scripts kept strictly apart, one that only reads and one that writes, and its own list of allowed calls. It builds on Chapter 1's register and Chapter 3's decisions, and refuses either if its hash doesn't match. Then four reviewed files. The first gives each re-platformed account's new account, workload and hosts, and the SailPoint request and ServiceNow change that approved its writes; Chapter 12 writes these rows for approved requests. The second says who may fetch each managed password. The third is the forest's account standard: the service account O U, the holding O U, the creator group, the retriever group pattern and the thirty-day interval. The fourth is the one program dates file. The end state has no default: if the decision is missing, the run stops rather than guessing.`,
      detail: `This chapter's code is powershell/NpiKerberosIsland/NpiKerberosIsland.psm1, two entry scripts (Invoke-NpiKerberosIsland.ps1, which only reads, and Invoke-NpiKerberosIslandAction.ps1, the write path), its own call list config/calls-ch06.json with the kerberos-island and kerberos-apply tasks, Chapter 03's decisions and four reviewed inputs. It builds on Chapter 01's register and NpiCore's findings, hashes and fixtures, and adds a fake Active Directory for this chapter's cmdlets. Inputs: Chapter 01's run folder, kept by the pipeline: register.json and manifest.json; the run refuses a register whose SHA-256 isn't the manifest's. Chapter 03's run folder, kept by the pipeline: decisions.json and manifest.json: each account's D-NP3 Disposition (Retire, Replatform, Keep, KeepKerberos) and Target, and for a kept account its EndDate, SpnOwner and the authorizing official's acceptance (AoAcceptedBy); the run refuses a decisions.json whose SHA-256 isn't the manifest's, or one decided on a different register. kerberos-replatform.csv, kept by application owners through identity engineering: per account Chapter 03 re-platforms, NewAccount, Workload (Service, IisAppPool or ScheduledTask) and Hosts for a gMSA or a dMSA, or Reference for a workload identity; the new account's SpnOwnerUpn and EndDate; then HostChangedOn and MigrationStartedOn as the work happens; OutboundOnlyReason when a new gMSA for an account with no SPN needs inbound authentication; and RequestId (the SailPoint request) and ChangeRecord (the ServiceNow change) that approved the account's writes. A kept account's SPN owner and end date are Chapter 03's. Chapter 12 writes the rows for approved requests. password-retrievers.json, kept by server and AD engineering, reviewed: per gMSA and dMSA, the distinguished names allowed to retrieve its password: for a gMSA exactly one DL-gMSA-<app>-Hosts group (WD-160; Learn recommends a security group of the hosts), for a dMSA the hosts it learned. requests names the SailPoint request and ServiceNow change behind each retriever change. ad-standard.json, kept by AD engineering, reviewed, one per forest: the standard new accounts follow: serviceAccountOu (where New-ADServiceAccount -Path creates them), holdingOu (where a disabled superseded account moves), creatorGroup (DL-gMSA-Creators, delegated the create right on the OU), retrieverGroupPattern (the only shape a gMSA's retriever may take) and passwordIntervalDays (30). A missing or malformed file stops the run. program-dates.json, kept by the identity engineering lead from the program master schedule: the one program dates file, which Chapter 03 also reads: D08's end date (d08LegacyAuthEndDate), G5's date (g5AdRetired) and D11's decision (d11: AdRetired, or KerberosIsland with an end of life). There is no default; a missing or unknown decision stops the run, and so does a copy whose SHA-256 isn't the one Chapter 03's run recorded.`,
