      sources: "Sources: examples/non-person-identity fixtures (fictional). Chapter 06, “Lab 1: fixtures, no tenant,” steps 6–9 and expected result.",
      say: `Next you read the island list and the reconciliation side by side. Two rows aren't ready for hand-off, so the flag stays false. The web front end carries the authorizing official's acceptance, so it becomes ready once it has an S P N owner. That's the point: an incomplete list never goes. Then the write path. A dry run as the pipeline reports four writes it would send, and refuses the migration's completion, a person's step. Applying it to the fake as the pipeline sends those four; applying it as a person sends the completion and refuses the rest. Each run reads every write back. Finally you pass a hash nobody approved, and it refuses outright. The full suite is five hundred forty-nine passing tests.`,
      detail: `Step 6, read the island list and the reconciliation. Import-Csv out/kerberos/island-list.csv | Format-Table Account, Type, Disposition, SpnOwnerUpn, EndDate, AoAcceptedBy, ReadyForP46. Island list: dmsa-hrsync$ dMSA Replatform hr-apps.owner@contoso.example 2027-06-30 True; gmsa-intranet$ gMSA Replatform (no owner) 2028-12-31 False; gmsa-ops$ gMSA Keep (no owner) 2027-08-31 True; gmsa-print$ gMSA Replatform print.owner@contoso.example 2027-06-30 True; gmsa-web$ gMSA Keep erin@contoso.example 2027-08-31 True; svc-webfront Traditional KeepKerberos (no owner) 2027-05-31 ao@contoso.example False. svc-webfront carries Chapter 03's acceptance by the authorizing official, so it becomes ready once it has an SPN owner; a KeepKerberos account without the acceptance stays not ready. Reconciliation: dmsa-hrsync$ dMSA, not planned, Island, OnIslandList; gmsa-ops$ gMSA, not planned, Island, OnIslandList; gmsa-sql$ gMSA, HandedOff; gmsa-web$ gMSA, Island, OnIslandList; gmsa-intranet$ gMSA, planned, Island, Unreconciled; gmsa-print$ gMSA, planned, Island, OnIslandList. Step 7, dry-run the plan with its approved hash, as the pipeline: $sha = (Get-FileHash out/kerberos/kerberos-plan.json -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLower(), then ./Invoke-NpiKerberosIslandAction.ps1 -PlanPath out/kerberos/kerberos-plan.json -ApprovedSha256 $sha -RunAs Pipeline -FixturePath tests/fixtures/estate.json -KerberosFixturePath tests/fixtures/ch06/ad-kerberos.json -OutputPath out/kerberos-dryrun. Output: DryRun as Pipeline: 1 Refused, 4 WouldSend: WouldSend pipeline SetRetrievers gmsa-ops$ Set-ADServiceAccount; WouldSend pipeline SetRetrievers gmsa-web$ Set-ADServiceAccount; WouldSend pipeline CreateGmsa msa-print01$ New-ADServiceAccount; Refused person CompleteDmsaMigration svc-hrsync Complete-ADServiceAccountMigration; WouldSend pipeline CreateGmsa svc-intranet New-ADServiceAccount. The dMSA's completion is a person's step, so the pipeline refuses it; the other four are the pipeline's. Step 8, apply it to the fake, the pipeline's steps then a person's (-RunAs Pipeline -Apply, then -RunAs Person -Apply with -OutputPath out/kerberos-apply-person), then try a hash nobody approved (-ApprovedSha256 ('0' * 64) -RunAs Pipeline): Apply as Pipeline: 1 Refused, 4 Sent, then Apply as Person: 4 Refused, 1 Sent: each run sends only its own writes, re-reads each one, and records Sent only when AD shows the change. results.json in each folder tells the operator what comes next (install each gMSA on its hosts and record HostChangedOn; the old svc-hrsync is disabled and kept) and carries each write's request, change record, controls and KSIs. Then the refusal: "The plan's SHA-256 is 40212250…, not the approved 0000…; refusing to run a plan nobody reviewed." Step 9, Invoke-Pester -Path tests. Expected result: 567 tests passing across the example (Tests Passed: 567), out/kerberos/manifest.json listing five AD reads and the SHA-256 of the register, of Chapter 03's decisions and of each input, ad-standard.json among them, and out/kerberos-apply/manifest.json listing the prechecks and re-reads and the pipeline's four writes, with runAs Pipeline; out/kerberos-apply-person/manifest.json lists the person's one. Delete out when you're done. The chapter's tests: tests/KerberosIslandSamples.Tests.ps1, 17 tests (Learn's procedures, run as published against stand-ins); tests/NpiKerberosIsland.Tests.ps1, 56 tests: the call list and its least-privilege check, the reader's refusals, the register's hash, Chapter 03's run and the one program dates file, ad-standard.json and the new columns, the end state with no default, the KDS root key read, each rule, the name standard and the one-group retriever rule, gMSA and dMSA paths step by step, outbound-only, the retrievers, the disable and the move to the holding OU, the island list and its hand-off, the reconciliation under each D11 decision, the deterministic plan, a write with no request or change record (NPI-KRB-10), a create with no tags or tags naming another request or change (WD-175), the writer's refusals (hash, tenant, domain, age, delete, undeclared call, parameter, empty list, encryption type, distinguished name, OU, interval, tags), -RunAs and who sends what, dry run, -Apply and the re-read, the prechecks that skip a write, and the entry scripts.`,
      cite: [],
